sentences.l:
But you, in the gathering seated, watch
the horses; and they, quickly, themselves straining for victory,
will come here;

And now he would have given him a horse, for the Achaeans approved,
if Antilochus, son of great-hearted Nestor,
had not answered Peleus’-son Achilles rightly, standing up:

for you are about to take away the contest-prize,
with those thoughts, that for him his chariot and two swift horses were hampered,
and he himself being good;

But if you pity him, and he has become dear to you in spirit,
there is in your hut much gold, and there is bronze
and sheep, and there are for you maids and solid-hoofed horses;

I will give him the breastplate, which from Asteropaeus I took away,
the bronze one, around which a pouring of shining tin
has been made to run round;

and the herald put in
his hand the sceptre, and bade be silent
the Argives;
